DIAGNOSTIC ONLY -- not a fix, not meant to stay. Logs the guest PC/RA at
sceKernelCreateLwMutex's success path, at sceKernelLockLwMutex's "Bad
workarea pointer" rejection, at sceKernelCreateThread's ILLEGAL_PRIORITY
rejection, and at Core/MemFault.cpp's "Bad memory access detected and
ignored" path, so the exact PSP EBOOT instruction responsible can be
resolved against build-psp/'s own ELF (psp-addr2line / objdump) instead of
guessing from symptoms alone.

The sceKernelCreateThread hook was added after a first round of testing
with the app-side pthread_init() workaround (see app/psp/main.cxx's
prime_pthread_init(), ADR 0047's bug 12): that workaround stopped the
originally-reported hang/crash from happening on the very first pthread
this port creates, but a second, later pthread_create() -- not called
from this project's own code, since LVGL's pthread OSAL is inactive
(LV_USE_OS is LV_OS_NONE here); most likely pspsdk's own lazily-spawned
fdman I/O-manager thread, triggered by the first real file open -- still
hits the same class of corrupted-argument symptom, this time an
out-of-range thread priority instead of a bad LwMutex workarea pointer.
This hook exists to identify the guest code creating that second thread.

Context: a real, reproducible crash (see docs/adr/0047-psp-memory-budget.md)
where this repo's PSP EBOOT boots on both interactive PPSSPP (`nix build
'.#ppsspp'`, real GUI/Vulkan) and real hardware (ARK-5 CFW) but never
reaches the title screen -- PPSSPP hangs after logging a real (non-poison)
"ACCESS_ERROR=sceKernelLockLwMutex(...): Bad workarea pointer for LwMutex"
plus ~150 "Bad memory access" hits in a small heap region just before it;
real hardware crashes back to the game list instead of hanging, consistent
with the same underlying bad pointer causing a genuine CPU exception there.
Headless PPSSPP (used for this repo's own psp-smoke CI and this ADR's
earlier bring-up verification) never reproduces it, so this needs a real
interactive run to capture.

Remove this patch (and revert flake.nix's reference to it) once the root
cause is found -- it exists only to get one more diagnostic data point out
of an interactive `nix build '.#ppsspp'` run.

